.TH SESSION 2
.SH NAME
session \- what a person did to a session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "session.m";
session := load Session Session->PATH;

Atype, Akey, Aclick, Adouble, Adrag, Amove, Await, Aresize, Apause: con iota;

Action: adt {
    kind:   int;
    at:     int;
    text:   string;
    x, y:   int;
    x2, y2: int;
    b:      int;

    script: fn(a: self ref Action): string;
    play:   fn(a: self ref Action): string;
};

init:  fn(): string;
read:  fn(path: string): (array of ref Action, string);
parse: fn(words: list of string): (ref Action, string);
span:  fn(a: array of ref Action): int;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
A recording made by
.B INFERNO_INPUT_RECORD
(see
.IR emu (1))
is key presses and releases and pointer states.
It reproduces a session exactly and reads like nothing at all.
.B Session
turns it into
.IR actions :
typed this, clicked there, waited.
.PP
.B Read
decodes a recording.
.B Script
renders one action as a line of shell, and
.B parse
reads such a line back, so a script edited by hand is understood by the same
code that wrote it.
.B Play
performs an action.
.B Span
is how long the whole recording lasts, in milliseconds.
.B Write
is the inverse of
.BR read :
actions back into a recording.
.PP
Decoding and writing are exact inverses on anything decoding produced.
A recording read, written and read again gives the identical actions, and a
script printed, compiled and printed again is the identical script \(em
checked by
.IR sessiontest (1).
The one thing that changes is the time before the first action, which a
recording has and a script does not.
.SS "What the decoding does"
Releases are dropped.
Runs of printable keys become one
.B Atype
with the text.
A press and release of a button at one place is a click; two of those within
500ms is a double click; one with movement between is a drag.
Movement with no button held is reported only where it stops.
Some of it is kept rather than none because a widget that tracks the pointer,
such as a menu, would otherwise never see it move \(em though no script here
has yet driven a menu, so that is the reason for keeping it rather than a
demonstration that this much is enough.
Quiet longer than 250ms becomes an explicit wait.
.SS "How an action is performed"
Through the two files the system already has for it:
.B /dev/keyboard
which has always accepted written keys, and
.B /dev/pointerin
(see
.IR pointer (3)).
Both are below every bind, so this drives
.IR wm (1)
and its clients and anything they started.
.PP
A click is written with explicit times rather than left to the clock, because a
consumer decides a double click from the difference between the times the
events carry.
The delivery is spaced to match those times as well.
Stamps alone are not enough: a press and a release written back to back arrive
microseconds apart however they are stamped, and a widget then sees a button
that was never really held.
That lost about one click in five against a real Tk button, and nothing but a
widget would have shown it \(em at the device the events are all present and
correctly stamped either way.
See
.IR clicktest (1),
which also establishes that a scripted click does work a Tk button, twenty-five
times out of twenty-five.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/session.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh-session (1),
.IR emu (1),
.IR pointer (3),
.IR cons (3)
.SH BUGS
A session driven by a script is not recorded.
.B /dev/pointerin
and writes to
.B /dev/keyboard
reach the non-recording half of
.B mousetrack
and
.BR gkbdputc ,
deliberately: a program inside the session that writes keys \(em
.IR keyuptest (1)
is one \(em runs again on a replay and writes them again, so recording them as
well would deliver them twice.
.PP
That is a choice rather than an obstacle, and it is narrower than it looks: the
doubling happens only when the replay re-runs the program that did the
injecting.
It is
.B write
rather than a recording flag that closes the loop, and it closes it better,
because it needs no replay at all and cannot drift: a recording becomes a
script, the script is edited, and this makes it a recording again.
.PP
.B Aresize
decodes but cannot be performed: the host owns the window size.
A recording that resized the window replays into whatever size the window
already has, and since pointer coordinates are absolute, the clicks then land
somewhere else.
.PP
A key release cannot be told from the German dead keys
.RB ( Grave ,
.BR Acute ,
.BR Circumflex ).
.B Keyup
is
.B Spec|0x800
and German is
.BR Spec|0xf00 ,
so the ranges overlap.
That is in the encoding rather than here, and it means a German keyboard's dead
keys decode as releases and vanish.
.PP
It reads what arrived, not what was meant.
A key held down long enough to repeat is several presses and decodes as
several characters, which is what the system saw.
